            {
              "id": "dfdf5ae1-babc-3772-9f70-5fe37b9f2665",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe \"Prohibited C Function Identified\" vulnerability refers to the use of C functions that are considered unsafe due to their potential to cause buffer overflows, format string vulnerabilities, or other security issues. In this case, the `fopen` function is used, which can lead to vulnerabilities if the file name is not properly validated or if the file is not properly handled after opening.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to use safer alternatives to `fopen` whenever possible. If `fopen` must be used, ensure that the file name is properly validated and that the file is properly handled after opening. This includes checking that the file was successfully opened and closing the file when it is no longer needed.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a safer alternative using C++ file streams:\n\n```cpp\n#include <fstream>\n\nstd::ifstream file(binaryFilename, std::ios::binary);\nif (!file) {\n    // Handle error\n}\n```\n\nIn this code, `std::ifstream` is used instead of `fopen`. This automatically handles file closing and can be used with C++ exception handling for error checking.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library:\n\n* `<fstream>`\n\n## References\n\n* [OWASP C++](https://owasp.org/www-pdf-archive/OWASP_SCP_v2.pdf)\n* [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)\n\nPlease note that the links provided are accessible to anonymous users at the time of writing this report."
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "f0207f86-9a77-3cbe-9c5c-9e5d2d300a07",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe \"Prohibited C Function Identified\" vulnerability refers to the use of C functions that are considered unsafe due to their potential to cause buffer overflows, format string vulnerabilities, or other security issues. In this case, the `fopen` function is used, which can lead to vulnerabilities if the file name is not properly validated or if the file is not properly handled after opening.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to use safer alternatives to `fopen` whenever possible. If `fopen` must be used, ensure that the file name is properly validated and that the file is properly handled after opening. This includes checking that the file was successfully opened and closing the file when it is no longer needed.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a safer alternative using C++ file streams:\n\n```cpp\n#include <fstream>\n\nstd::ifstream file(binaryFilename, std::ios::binary);\nif (!file) {\n    // Handle error\n}\n```\n\nIn this code, `std::ifstream` is used instead of `fopen`. This automatically handles file closing and can be used with C++ exception handling for error checking.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library:\n\n* `<fstream>`\n\n## References\n\n* [OWASP C++](https://owasp.org/www-pdf-archive/OWASP_SCP_v2.pdf)\n* [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)\n\nPlease note that the links provided are accessible to anonymous users at the time of writing this report."
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
