            {
              "id": "0bcd6213-13a4-3cc3-8735-de6b233968b6",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability in C++ refers to the use of insecure C library functions that can lead to various security issues such as buffer overflows, format string vulnerabilities, or integer overflows. In this case, the `fopen` function is used, which can lead to vulnerabilities if the file name or path is manipulated by an attacker.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to use safer alternatives to these functions. For instance, instead of `fopen`, use `fopen_s` which is a safer version. Always validate and sanitize inputs, especially if they are used to construct file names or paths. Avoid using user input directly in file operations.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace the `fopen` function with `fopen_s`:\n\n```cpp\nerrno_t err = fopen_s(&m_fpCompare, m_fileNameCompareCurrent, \"w\");\nif (err != 0) {\n    // Handle error\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library:\n\n- `stdio.h` for `fopen` function\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++](https://owasp.org/www-pdf-archive/OWASP_SCP_v2.pdf)\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "7d5dbfe7-a009-30ba-aebc-f2351b74b609",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability in C++ refers to the use of insecure C library functions that can lead to various security issues such as buffer overflows, format string vulnerabilities, integer overflows, etc. In this case, the `fopen` function is used, which can lead to vulnerabilities if the `fileName` parameter is not properly validated or controlled.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to use safer alternatives to these functions. For instance, instead of `fopen`, you can use `fopen_s` which is a more secure version of `fopen`. Also, always validate and sanitize inputs to these functions to prevent security issues.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a recommended fix for the code:\n\n```cpp\nerrno_t err = fopen_s(&m_fpWrite, fileName, \"wb+\");\nif (err != 0) {\n    // Handle error\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library:\n\n```cpp\n#include <stdio.h>\n```\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++](https://owasp.org/www-pdf-archive/OWASP_SCP_v2.pdf)\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)\n\nPlease note that the links provided are active and accessible to anonymous users at the time of writing this report."
