            {
              "id": "81b802cc-c7a5-30e9-918c-4c25273e539b",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `strlen` function in C and C++ is used to find the length of a string. However, it can lead to vulnerabilities if not used properly. The function counts the number of characters in a string up to but not including the null-terminating character. If the string is not null-terminated, `strlen` will continue reading memory until it encounters a null byte, potentially leading to buffer overflows or other undefined behavior.\n\nIn the provided code snippet, the vulnerability lies in the assumption that `fileName` is a null-terminated string. If `fileName` is not null-terminated, `strlen(fileName)` could lead to a buffer overflow.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, always ensure that strings are null-terminated before passing them to `strlen`. Additionally, consider using safer alternatives to `strlen` that take a maximum length parameter, such as `strnlen`, to prevent buffer overflows.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nA safer version of the code snippet might look like this:\n\n```cpp\n#include <cstring> // for strnlen\n\nsize_t len = strnlen(fileName, MAX_LENGTH);\nextpos = len > 0 ? (int)len - 1 : 0;\n```\n\nIn this version, `strnlen` is used instead of `strlen`, and `MAX_LENGTH` is the maximum expected length of `fileName`. This prevents `strnlen` from reading past the end of `fileName` if it is not null-terminated.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the `<cstring>` library for the `strnlen` function.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C/C++ Vulnerabilities](https://owasp.org/www-pdf-archive/OWASP_SCP_v2.pdf)\n- [CWE-126: Buffer Over-read](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/126.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS140021"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "433a6866-dd42-3749-9b0c-afa4a1365509",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Prohibited C Function Found\" is a vulnerability that arises when a C++ program uses a function that is considered unsafe or insecure. These functions are often prohibited because they can lead to serious security issues such as buffer overflows, format string vulnerabilities, or integer overflows. In this case, the `fopen` function is used, which can lead to vulnerabilities if the `fileName` parameter is not properly validated or sanitized.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to use safer alternatives to the `fopen` function. If you must use `fopen`, ensure that the `fileName` parameter is properly validated and sanitized to prevent potential security issues. \n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nA safer alternative to `fopen` could be `fopen_s`, which is a more secure version of `fopen`. Here is how you could use it:\n\n```cpp\nerrno_t err;\nFILE* fp;\nerr = fopen_s(&fp, fileName, \"r\");\nif (err != 0) {\n    // Handle error\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library:\n\n* `stdio.h` - for the `fopen` function\n\n## OWASP Resources\n\n* [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices Guide](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n\n## Common Weakness Enumeration (CWE)\n\n* [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "fd010c8a-e2c4-3c54-b182-0c3ab803f569",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is considered a genuine vulnerability because the analysis detected a possible undefined behavior risk due to overlapping memory regions between the source and destination in a memory copy operation. The destination and source expressions are derived from potentially related base pointers, and dataflow analysis suggests that overlap is feasible. Additionally, there is no explicit check or guard to prevent this overlap, and the source pointer could also be null, which further increases the risk. The classification specifically highlights undefined behavior due to possible overlap, which is a well-known issue with memory copy functions like memcpy. No evidence was found to suggest that this is a false positive, and the verdict is marked as genuine.\n\n## In Context Remediation\nTo prevent undefined behavior when the source and destination memory regions may overlap, replace the memory copy operation with a function that safely handles overlapping regions, such as `memmove`. This ensures that the copy is performed correctly even if the regions overlap.\n\n```cpp\n// Replace memcpy with memmove to safely handle possible overlap\nmemmove(pMat->data + y * pMat->step, src + y * addr.stride_y, rowSize);\n```\n\nAlternatively, if you can guarantee that the source and destination never overlap, add an explicit assertion or check before the copy to document and enforce this assumption:\n\n```cpp\n// Add a runtime check to ensure no overlap\nassert((pMat->data + y * pMat->step + rowSize <= src + y * addr.stride_y) ||\n       (src + y * addr.stride_y + rowSize <= pMat->data + y * pMat->step));\nmemcpy(pMat->data + y * pMat->step, src + y * addr.stride_y, rowSize);\n```\n\n## References\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n- [CWE-787: Out-of-bounds Write](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/787.html)\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n\n___\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS121708"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "bc07d845-70dc-39f2-a235-d19ce8c2991b",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is classified as a genuine vulnerability because the code uses a memory copy operation where the source and destination memory regions may overlap. The analysis detected that the source and destination expressions are related by data flow, which means there is a realistic risk that they could refer to overlapping memory. This can result in undefined behavior according to the C standard for memcpy. There are no explicit checks or guards in the code to prevent this overlap, and the types and capacity of the buffers are not clearly defined, increasing the risk. The source and destination are not null, so null pointer dereference is not a concern here. The risk is specifically about undefined behavior due to overlapping memory regions during the copy operation.\n\n## In Context Remediation\nTo prevent undefined behavior when the source and destination memory regions may overlap, replace the memory copy operation with a function that safely handles overlapping regions, such as `memmove`. `memmove` is designed to correctly handle overlapping memory areas, unlike `memcpy` which can cause data corruption or crashes in such cases. \n\n**Remediation:**\n\n```cpp\nmemmove(dst + y * addr.stride_y, pMat->data + y * pMat->step, rowSize);\n```\n\nThis change ensures that the copy operation is safe even if the source and destination regions overlap.\n\n## References\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n- [CWE-787: Out-of-bounds Write](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/787.html)\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n\n___\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS121708"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "a0212388-f7aa-3d0b-ba29-8a1bbba3019f",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Prohibited C Function Found\" is a vulnerability that occurs when a C++ program uses a function that is considered unsafe or insecure. These functions are often prohibited because they can lead to serious security issues such as buffer overflows, format string vulnerabilities, or integer overflows.\n\nIn the provided code snippet, the `fopen` function is used, which is a common function in C++ for opening a file. However, it does not check if the file opening operation was successful or not. If the file does not exist or cannot be opened for some reason (e.g., insufficient permissions), the function will return a null pointer, which can lead to undefined behavior if not handled properly.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to always check the return value of `fopen` and handle the error appropriately. This can prevent undefined behavior and potential security issues.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a recommended fix for the provided code snippet:\n\n```cpp\nm_fpRead = fopen(fileName, \"rb\");\nif (m_fpRead == NULL) {\n    // Handle error appropriately\n    // For example, print an error message and exit the program\n    std::cerr << \"Failed to open file: \" << fileName << std::endl;\n    exit(EXIT_FAILURE);\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe provided code snippet requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `stdio.h` or `cstdio` for the `fopen` function\n- `iostream` for the `std::cerr` object\n- `stdlib.h` or `cstdlib` for the `exit` function and `EXIT_FAILURE` constant\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "4f8a5aa2-e81c-3d60-941d-d07a942469e6",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is classified as a genuine vulnerability because the code uses a memory copy operation where the source and destination memory regions may overlap. The analysis detected that the destination and source expressions are related by data flow, which means there is a realistic risk that they could refer to overlapping memory areas. This can lead to undefined behavior according to the C standard, as memcpy is not safe for overlapping regions. There are no explicit checks or guards in the code to prevent this, and the analysis did not find evidence that the source or destination pointers are null, so the main risk is the potential for memory overlap. The probability of this being a false positive is low, as the overlap was determined via data flow analysis and not by superficial pattern matching.\n\n## In Context Remediation\nTo prevent undefined behavior when copying memory regions that may overlap, replace the use of `memcpy` with `memmove`, which is designed to handle overlapping memory safely. The function call should remain the same, but the function name should be changed:\n\n```cpp\nmemmove(dst + y * addr.stride_y, pMat->data + y * pMat->step, rowSize);\n```\n\nThis change ensures that the copy operation is safe even if the source and destination regions overlap, eliminating the risk of undefined behavior.\n\n## References\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n- [CWE-787: Out-of-bounds Write](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/787.html)\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n\n___\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS121708"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "5e9d1c75-42e6-301e-b0e7-4bad3f58cb90",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability in C++ programming language refers to the use of certain C standard library functions that are known to be dangerous due to their potential to cause buffer overflows, format string vulnerabilities, or other problems. One such function is `printf`.\n\nThe `printf` function is a common function in C and C++ for outputting text. However, it can be dangerous if used improperly, especially when handling user-supplied input. This is because `printf` can interpret certain special characters (like `%s`, `%d`, etc.) and if an attacker can control the format string, they can potentially exploit this to execute arbitrary code, read from or write to arbitrary memory locations, or cause the program to crash.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, avoid using `printf` with user-supplied input. Instead, use safer alternatives that do not interpret format strings, such as `puts` or `fputs`. If you need to format strings, use functions like `snprintf` that limit the amount of output, and always use a static format string.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nInstead of:\n\n```cpp\nchar* user_input = get_user_input();\nprintf(user_input);\n```\n\nUse:\n\n```cpp\nchar* user_input = get_user_input();\nputs(user_input);\n```\n\nOr, if you need to format strings:\n\n```cpp\nchar* user_input = get_user_input();\nchar buffer[256];\nsnprintf(buffer, sizeof(buffer), \"%s\", user_input);\nputs(buffer);\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code examples above require the following library:\n\n```cpp\n#include <stdio.h>\n```\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "8a4905e1-5b7e-39a8-a9e1-e4e20f6cc6dd",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability in C++ programming language refers to the use of certain C standard library functions that are known to be dangerous due to their potential to cause buffer overflows, format string vulnerabilities, or other problems. One such function is `printf`.\n\nThe `printf` function is a common function in C and C++ for outputting text. However, it can be dangerous if used improperly, especially when handling user-supplied input. This is because `printf` can interpret certain special characters (like `%s`, `%d`, etc.) and if an attacker can control the format string, they can potentially exploit this to execute arbitrary code, read from or write to arbitrary memory locations, or cause the program to crash.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, avoid using `printf` with user-supplied input. Instead, use safer alternatives that do not interpret format strings, such as `puts` or `fputs`. If you need to format strings, use functions like `snprintf` that limit the amount of output, and always use a static format string.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nInstead of:\n\n```cpp\nchar* user_input = get_user_input();\nprintf(user_input);\n```\n\nUse:\n\n```cpp\nchar* user_input = get_user_input();\nputs(user_input);\n```\n\nOr, if you need to format strings:\n\n```cpp\nchar* user_input = get_user_input();\nchar buffer[256];\nsnprintf(buffer, sizeof(buffer), \"%s\", user_input);\nputs(buffer);\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code examples above require the following library:\n\n```cpp\n#include <stdio.h>\n```\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "26d66afd-aec6-358c-b781-6c0e27d15515",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability refers to the use of insecure C library functions in C++ code. These functions are often prone to buffer overflow attacks, format string vulnerabilities, or other types of security issues. In the provided code snippet, the `fopen` function is used, which can lead to vulnerabilities if not used properly.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to use safer alternatives to these functions. In the case of `fopen`, it is recommended to use C++ file I/O operations instead of C-style I/O operations. \n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace the `fopen` function with the `ifstream` function from the C++ standard library. Here is how you can do it:\n\n```cpp\n#include <fstream>\n\nstd::ifstream m_fpRead(m_fileNameRead.c_str(), std::ios::binary);\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `<fstream>`: This is a standard C++ library for input/output stream class to operate on files.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++](https://owasp.org/www-pdf-archive/OWASP_SCP_v2.pdf)\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)\n\nPlease note that the links provided are active and accessible to anonymous users at the time of writing this report."
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "30302c3e-c060-315c-92d8-9630c77f0258",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `strcpy` function in C++ is a standard library function that copies a string. However, it is considered unsafe because it does not check the size of the destination buffer, which can lead to buffer overflow vulnerabilities if the source string is larger than the destination buffer. This can lead to crashes, data corruption, and potentially code execution, making it a serious security risk.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should use safer string copy functions that include a length parameter. This allows the function to ensure that it does not write past the end of the buffer. Functions like `strncpy`, `strlcpy`, or `memcpy` can be used instead, but they must be used correctly to ensure safety.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a fixed version of the code using `strncpy`:\n\n```cpp\nstrncpy(m_cameraName, fileName, sizeof(m_cameraName) - 1);\nm_cameraName[sizeof(m_cameraName) - 1] = '\\0';\n```\n\nThis code will copy the string from `fileName` to `m_cameraName`, but it will not write past the end of `m_cameraName`. The `- 1` is to leave space for the null terminator, which is then manually added on the next line.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the `<cstring>` library.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS185832"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "7bb1eef4-67ec-3880-89a2-9fbc9e2dbaba",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability in C++ programming language refers to the use of certain C standard library functions that are known to be dangerous due to their potential to cause buffer overflows, format string vulnerabilities, or other problems. One such function is `printf`.\n\nThe `printf` function is a common function in C and C++ for outputting text. However, it can be dangerous if used improperly, especially when handling user-supplied input. This is because `printf` can interpret certain special characters (like `%s`, `%d`, etc.) and if an attacker can control the format string, they can potentially exploit this to execute arbitrary code, read from or write to arbitrary memory locations, or cause the program to crash.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, avoid using `printf` with user-supplied input. Instead, use safer alternatives that do not interpret format strings, such as `puts` or `fputs`. If you need to format strings, use functions like `snprintf` that limit the amount of output, and always use a static format string.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nInstead of:\n\n```cpp\nchar* user_input = get_user_input();\nprintf(user_input);\n```\n\nUse:\n\n```cpp\nchar* user_input = get_user_input();\nputs(user_input);\n```\n\nOr, if you need to format strings:\n\n```cpp\nchar* user_input = get_user_input();\nchar buffer[256];\nsnprintf(buffer, sizeof(buffer), \"%s\", user_input);\nputs(buffer);\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code examples above require the following library:\n\n```cpp\n#include <stdio.h>\n```\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "1be246b9-e1fb-32f1-92bd-76d08809cf93",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe vulnerability in this code snippet is related to the `strlen` function in C++. The `strlen` function is used to find the length of a string, but it does not check for null termination. If the string is not null-terminated, `strlen` will continue reading until it encounters a null character, potentially leading to a buffer overflow. This can lead to undefined behavior, including program crashes, data corruption, and other security vulnerabilities.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should always ensure that your strings are null-terminated. This can be done by initializing your strings with a null character or by manually adding a null character at the end of your string. Additionally, you should avoid using functions like `strlen` that do not check for null termination. Instead, use safer alternatives that include bounds checking, such as `strnlen`.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a safer version of the code snippet:\n\n```cpp\n#include <cstring>\n#include <string>\n\nstd::string fileNameStr(fileName);\nif (fileNameStr.find(\"%\") == std::string::npos && \n    (!_stricmp(&fileNameStr[fileNameStr.length() - 4], \".avi\") || \n    !_stricmp(&fileNameStr[extpos], \".mp4\"))) {\n    // ...\n}\n```\n\nIn this version, we convert the C string to a C++ string, which is safer because it automatically includes a null character at the end. We then use the `std::string::find` function instead of `strstr` to search for the \"%\" character. This function returns `std::string::npos` if the character is not found, so we check for this value to determine if the \"%\" character is not in the string.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code snippet requires the following libraries:\n\n- `<cstring>`: for the `_stricmp` function.\n- `<string>`: for the `std::string` class.\n\n## OWASP and CWE Links\n\n- [OWASP C++](https://owasp.org/www-pdf-archive/OWASP_SCP_Quick_Reference_Guide_v2.pdf)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS140021"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "ed7ac48c-1258-3ebb-bc53-18967dfe6cd1",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `strlen` function in C and C++ is used to find the length of a string. However, it can lead to vulnerabilities if not used properly. The function counts the number of characters in a string up to but not including the null-terminating character. If the string is not null-terminated, `strlen` will continue reading memory until it encounters a null byte, potentially leading to buffer overflows or other undefined behavior.\n\nIn the provided code snippet, the vulnerability lies in the assumption that `fileName` is a null-terminated string. If `fileName` is not null-terminated, `strlen(fileName)` could lead to a buffer overflow.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, always ensure that strings are null-terminated before passing them to `strlen`. Additionally, consider using safer alternatives to `strlen` that take a maximum length parameter, such as `strnlen`, to prevent buffer overflows.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nA safer version of the code snippet might look like this:\n\n```cpp\n#include <cstring> // for strnlen\n\nsize_t len = strnlen(fileName, MAX_LENGTH);\nextpos = len > 0 ? (int)len - 1 : 0;\n```\n\nIn this version, `strnlen` is used instead of `strlen`, and `MAX_LENGTH` is the maximum expected length of `fileName`. This prevents `strnlen` from reading past the end of `fileName` if it is not null-terminated.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the `<cstring>` library for the `strnlen` function.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C/C++ Vulnerabilities](https://owasp.org/www-pdf-archive/OWASP_SCP_v2.pdf)\n- [CWE-126: Buffer Over-read](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/126.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS140021"
                ]
              }
