            {
              "id": "5ce6aee6-8626-341d-9c0c-64606afc759b",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability refers to the use of insecure C library functions in C++ code. These functions are often prone to buffer overflow attacks, format string vulnerabilities, and other security issues. In this case, the `qsort` function is used, which is not inherently insecure, but can lead to vulnerabilities if not used correctly.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to use C++ standard library functions and classes instead of C library functions. The C++ standard library provides many functions and classes that are safer and more efficient than their C counterparts. \n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nIn this case, you can replace the `qsort` function with the `std::sort` function from the C++ standard library. Here is how you can do it:\n\n```cpp\n#include <algorithm>\n\n// ...\n\nstd::sort(pixelArr, pixelArr + 9);\n```\n\nNote that the `compareTwo` function is not needed when using `std::sort` with an array of `unsigned char`, because `std::sort` can automatically compare `unsigned char` values. If you need a custom comparison function, you can provide it as the third argument to `std::sort`.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `<stdlib.h>` for the `qsort` function\n- `<algorithm>` for the `std::sort` function\n\n## OWASP Resources\n\n\n## Common Weakness Enumeration (CWE)\n\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "df2478b5-3512-3d55-956c-e236718fe668",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability refers to the use of insecure C library functions in C++ code. These functions are often prone to buffer overflow attacks, format string vulnerabilities, and other security issues. In this case, the `qsort` function is used, which is not inherently insecure, but can lead to vulnerabilities if not used correctly.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to use C++ standard library functions and classes instead of C library functions. The C++ standard library provides many functions and classes that are safer and more efficient than their C counterparts. \n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nIn this case, you can replace the `qsort` function with the `std::sort` function from the C++ standard library. Here is how you can do it:\n\n```cpp\n#include <algorithm>\n\n// ...\n\nstd::sort(pixelArr, pixelArr + 9);\n```\n\nNote that the `compareTwo` function is not needed when using `std::sort` with an array of `unsigned char`, because `std::sort` can automatically compare `unsigned char` values. If you need a custom comparison function, you can provide it as the third argument to `std::sort`.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `<stdlib.h>` for the `qsort` function\n- `<algorithm>` for the `std::sort` function\n\n## OWASP Resources\n\n\n## Common Weakness Enumeration (CWE)\n\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
